    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d178">

                <pb facs="0511" n="397" sameAs="frus:frus1943/pg397"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Roosevelt Papers: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Prime Minister Churchill to President Roosevelt
                    </hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d178-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">most secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-07-25">25 July 1943</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>378. Former Naval Person to President personal
                    and most secret.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>Operation 
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Quadrant</hi>. As Colonel Warden (see my immediately following) is
                        going by the same method as last time,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d178-f2"/> he
                        will have to arrive at “<hi
                                rendition="#small-caps">Abraham</hi>” during the 10th and
                        will await you there. I should be glad to know if your delay till the 17th
                        means that you are going to meet UJ. If this
                        is so and you are not taking your staff with you, it has occurred to me that
                        perhaps these might come to “<hi
                                rendition="#small-caps">Abraham</hi>” a few days earlier so
                        as to begin the discussions, which are always lengthy, with their opposite
                        number. On military grounds, we are very anxious that the staffs should be
                        in contact as soon as possible.</item>
                    <pb facs="0512" n="398" xml:id="pg398"/>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>Your 323.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d178-f3"/> I quite understand your position
                        and am supporting it by a message to Mackenzie King, which I am repeating to
                            you.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d178-f4"/> I expect that McCarthy will be able
                        to convince him, as he is very keen on the project.</item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>
                        Colonel Warden is bringing Mrs Warden with him as you have several times
                        kindly suggested, also Lieutenant Mary Warden<anchor n="5" xml:id="d178-f5"
                        /> as aide de camp. All three members of the Warden family are greatly
                        looking forward to the trip.</item>
                    <label>4.</label>
                    <item>The “<hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Husky</hi>” news is very good and will be better. I trust we
                        can keep all important options in this theatre open until we meet. I hope
                        you are not too lonely now that Congress has departed. Up to date in July,
                        we have caught 26 canaries,<anchor n="6" xml:id="d178-f6"/> which is good
                        for 25 days. There should be quite a good meal for our cats when the time
                        comes.</item>
                </list>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d178-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d178-f1-content">
                <p>Sent by the United States Military Attaché, London, via Army channels.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d178-f2" n="2" xml:id="d178-f2-content">
                <p>i.e., on the <hi rendition="#italic">Queen Mary.</hi>
                </p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d178-f3" n="3" xml:id="d178-f3-content">
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Supra.</hi>
                </p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d178-f4" n="4" xml:id="d178-f4-content">
                <p>See Churchill’s telegram No. 380 to Roosevelt, below.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d178-f5" n="5" xml:id="d178-f5-content">
                <p>Churchill’s daughter Mary.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d178-f6" n="6" xml:id="d178-f6-content">
                <p>i.e., sunk 26 enemy submarines.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
